          <div id="znid-745939249668"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>You’re cruising down a bike trail, and glance to your left to see a Pittsburgh &amp; Lake Erie train right beside you. But there aren’t any train tracks there; just a tangle of trees and vines. And this train isn’t made of metal. It’s made from thousands of pieces of grey and black tile.</p><p><br></p><p>Surprise. You’ve just discovered The Ruins.</p><p><br></p><p>Nestled in the woods 30 miles southeast of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, this unique art installation is comprised of dozens of mosaics and other art projects that honor the place’s history. For 52 years, the site was the Banning #2 coal mine, which employed over 600 miners in its heyday. </p><p><br></p><p>This retired mine has been given new life through the work of mosaic artist Rachel Sager, who has not only created a shining legacy to those who worked in the coal mines of southwestern Pennsylvania, but invited hundreds of artists from all over the world to do the same.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

