          <div id="znid-996607736023"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>The daughter, grand-daughter, and great-granddaughter of miners, Sager says she never felt any different than her peers…until she went to college. There, her classmates looked down on the mining industry. They treated coal like it was a four-letter word.</p><p><br></p><p>“It broke me inside,” said Sager. “Coal paid for my college.”</p><p><br></p><p>But Sager refused to feel shame about her past, and years later, she learned about an 11-acre property with a foreclosed home for sale. The home sat beside a former mine, complete with wall after wall made from concrete. “A mosaic artist finding a giant substrate to use as a canvas is the perfect storm,” laughs Sager.</p><p><br></p><p>By bringing her two worlds together — art and coal — she has opened up the stories of Appalachia and its coal-mining history to a much larger audience. Today, The Ruins attracts both national and international attention and not just from visitors. More than 500 artists from over 17 countries have contributed to the ever-evolving outdoor art project.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-351428494624"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="image" data-filter="none"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              
        <div class="zn-image">
          
          
          <img id="" class=" zn-nodrag" src="https://media.zenobuilder.com/upload/sp-9949/The-Ruins-Beehive-Photo-by-Rachel-Sager-copy-85510601.jpeg" alt="" draggable="false"    />
          
        </div>
      
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-874871458814"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="heading"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h5 id="" class="zn-heading "   ><i>RUINS BEEHIVE EXHIBIT. PHOTO BY VANESSA ORR.</i></h5>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-744400307008"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>One of the latest additions to this sprawling experiment is the Ruins Beehive. It alone was created by 200 artists, a lively mix of beginners and experts. Sager put out an open call, inviting artists to submit small hexagons, which came together in a beautiful piece of modern art. Because so many mosaic artists are self-taught and don’t have an art-school background, Sager designed the project to provide an education in color theory as part of an immersive, inclusive experience for the participants.</p><p><br></p><p>But perhaps what is most striking are Sager’s tributes to miners themselves, including an incredibly lifelike mosaic of her father, who owned Sager Coal Company, which operated at a number of sites in southwestern Pennsylvania.  Another group of artists is currently working on a coal miner portrait modeled on a present-day West Virginia mine worker. Together, these art pieces remind visitors that the coal industry is not just part of Appalachia’s past; it’s still alive today. </p><p><br></p><p>Sager is also writing a book about The Ruins, speaks to groups about her unique project, and offers guided tours of the site. They can be booked at www.sagermosaics.com.</p><p><br></p><p>“I just want to keep doing what I’m doing,” she says, referring to the project as her life’s mission. “The Ruins is about excellence in mosaic and great storytelling and honoring the people who worked here.”</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

